Original commit body from 8ff65322d28fc9d28768d8ffe7f1f7a42876f0fe:

INV-bonup (ADR-0041 §1): supply_chain_tier must name supply-chain DISTANCE only —
"no other fact may influence the value." Six protocol linkers (Tauri IPC,
wasm-bindgen, message-dispatch, @hg: annotations, Yjs CRDT, Solidity ABI) violated
this by stamping supply_chain_tier=2 on their synthetic stand-in nodes purely as a
side-effect trigger: _classify_symbols skips any symbol whose tier != 1, so the
linkers borrowed tier 2 as a "do-not-reclassify-by-host-path" lock rather than a
distance. That encoded a mechanism in the distance axis and mislabeled every one of
those nodes internal_dep.

Structural fix: key the skip on the honest signal for what these nodes actually are
— synthetic protocol stand-ins — via the protocol_origin field (ADR-0031; already
THE synthetic-stand-in marker at the cli read boundary, cli.py:6143/6255). All 14
tier=2 stamps across the 6 linkers are dropped; the nodes fall to the Symbol default
supply_chain_tier=1 (honest first-party distance) and _classify_symbols now skips
them via `or symbol.protocol_origin`. 13 of the 14 also carry a supply_chain_reason
(kept as provenance; already an independent skip trigger); solidity_abi's call-site
node — the lone stamp with no reason — is covered by the protocol_origin skip.

Behavior change (a correction): supply_chain_summary per-tier `symbols` counts shift
(these synthetic nodes move internal_dep→first_party); they are non-`file` kinds so
per-tier `files` counts are unaffected. Updated the 3 tests asserting the old tier==2
mechanism value (wasm/tauri linkers) to tier==1; added a TestClassifySymbolsPreservesTier
case proving a protocol_origin stand-in (no reason) stays tier 1 and unclassified.
The two remaining tier==2 assertions elsewhere are legitimate (IR round-trip;
workspace-internal dependency declarations) and untouched. Full suite green, changed
sources at 100% coverage.

